h  with most technologies for example with  EEG Zoar local field potentials you can  record across several days two weeks  however you don't have any single-cell  resolution you can you can record with  Tet Rhodes for example but and get  single-cell resolution in freely  behaving animals however you can't  record from these cells you usually over  many days to weeks it's very difficult  to do that plus you would like to be  able to identify the cell types to  record from and so one technique that  has been developed recently by by the  the schnitzer lab and later  commercialized as this these  miniaturized fluorescence microscopes  and we decided to to try to build these  microscopes ourselves and then to  improve on them to make the new  generation of these microscopes and then  to make them available to the community  so so how do you think about these types  of fluorescent microscopes so in essence  you should you can think about the the  microscopes that you have at the EPI  fluorescent microscopes that you may  have in your lab where you use them to  look at fixed issue for example  fluorescent fixed issue in essence these  miniaturize microscopes are exactly that  so you can you you shine light over the  the sample at a particular wavelength  and fluorescence comes back and you can  record this emitted light the technique  has several drawbacks compared to for  example two-photon microscopy  in the sense that you can out-of-focus  light is also image that and there's a  great deal of scattering however with  the new techniques that we've developed  these limitations are far the the  benefits are far outweigh the  limitations in the sense that you can  record in freely behaving animals with  it's really great great temporal  resolution and spatial resolution and  and we can record some identify it  selves and so you can take this emitted  light that comes back and you can look  at the activities of the cells that that  you get and so so imagine your that your 