s is  example hopefully this will play we're  having some difficulties here but what  you would see here is this animal  running back and forth on this track and  the cells flashing and below you can see  the Delta F over F calcium traces each  each time the cell bursts of active the  cell fires a burst of action potentials  you can see these deflections up and the  slow decay of the calcium transient and  you can see that this is a few of the  cells that are that are displayed but  you can typically record these from  several hundred cells and over several  days more than a thousand cells in one  hippocampal recording and the critical  thing is you'll be able to go back the  same cells day after day week after week  and record from the cells as long as the  the calcium indicator expression is  stable so we have tested in our lab we  have tested these microscopes in a model  of epilepsy we were in in in this  particular experiment we were interested  in epilepsy in a model of temporal lobe  epilepsy because epilepsy is associated  with cognitive dysfunction there are  these these people that have epilepsy  have a great deal of memory difficulties  it's important to understand the  mechanisms that are underlying this and  so in temple of epilepsy there's a lot  of reorganization of circuits a certain  vulnerability AI other other cell  populations try to adjust by sprouting  connections into places they normally  wouldn't and this results in a rewired  hippocampus and ultimately altered  encoding of memories and poor memory  retrieval it's this is a progressive  condition this will get worse and worse  as the epilepsy progresses so we decided  to try to understand how place cell  dynamics are altered in this model and  how more importantly how place so  stability is altered so we created a  model of epilepsy this is the  pilocarpine model where animals have a  prolonged seizure this two-hour seizure  is ended by an injection of diazepam  however after this prolonged 