e and the behavior of the  animals is quite excellent even though  this this particular version is a little  bit more than 4 grams the animals can be  trained to carry it and do the tasks and  so we're quite excited about this  development and hope to release it soon  in our open source version this is our  wiring schematic of the wireless  microscope and you can see the top view  where the micro SD card gets mounted and  the various components that which make  it possible the MCU that runs the whole  circuitry and the CMOS imaging sensor at  the bottom and so like I was saying we'd  like to go below 4 grams with the  battery we'd like to get more than 20  minutes of continuous recordings and 20  frames per second at least and you'd  like to be able to trigger the  microscope to turn on and turn off  through IR communication and we'd like  to also be able to make it so that we  can have a wireless microscope become  wired if we have other needs for that so  we'd like to be able to design that and  you're very close to completing this as  part of the project as part of the brain  initiative grant we also promise to  build several other miniature microscope  one was a and optogenetics capable  microscope and so this is a microscope  where you can not only do calcium  imaging but to be able to deliver  lights to turn on up or turn off cells  using optogenetics what we had in mind  was essentially a version where there's  instead of one LED there's two LEDs one  blue LED to excite channelrhodopsin and  another LED to excite this other calcium  indicator junior camp 1a where the where  the the emissions the excitations of the  two don't overlap and so we'd like to be  able to image the junior camp 1a at the  same time as we excite channelrhodopsin  and be able to see how this excitation  of whatever cells are expressing  channelrhodopsin alters the activity of  these cells so this is something we're  currently designing and building we know  that the junior camp 1a works